WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:03.820
Hey, Wisecrack.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:04.540
Jared, here.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.420
Today, we’re breaking down everyone’s
favorite insomniac, his soap-salesman BFF,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.889
and their super, super secret underground
club for dudes who like to beat each other

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:13.889
senseless.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:16.880
That’s right, we’re talking bout the thing
you’re not supposed to talk about.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.441
For most, Fight Club is about chiseled men
who like to knock each other’s teeth out

00:00:20.441 --> 00:00:24.750
and destroy private property to escape the
monotony of consumer culture.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.520
But there’s something about Fight Club we
often overlook.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.530
Is Tyler Durden really the suave savior we
thought he was?

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.890
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The Philosophy
of Fight Club.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.080
And as always, spoilers ahead.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.090
Fight Club begins with our nameless protagonist
slash narrator as he tries to beat insomnia

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.970
and complete his life one Ikea shelf at a
time.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:44.970
"Uh, yeah.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.760
I'd like to order the Erika Pekkari dust ruffles."

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.460
His doctor suggests he see real pain at a
testicular cancer support group, and our Narrator

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.040
finally finds the key to sleep: crying his
eyes out.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.430
But, another faker, Marla Singer, ruins it,
flaunting her lack of balls to our, now again,

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.460
sleepless narrator.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.260
"Testicular cancer should be no contest, I
think.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:04.940
Right?“

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.980
"Well, technically I have more of a right
to be there than you.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.360
You still have your balls."

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:12.679
All that gets put on hold when our narrator’s
apartment blows up, destroying his perfectly

00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:16.619
manicured lifestyle, and forcing him to live
with one Tyler Durden.

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:20.909
He and Tyler share some drinks, go on wild
and zany adventures, and convince lots of

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:22.659
people to beat the sh*t out of each other.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:24.299
Also, domestic terrorism.

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:25.479
But we’ll get to that.

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:29.780
The most overt theme in Fight Club is the
drudgery of consumer capitalism.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:34.119
Our narrator lives a ‘tiny life’ of tedium,
working a job he hates.

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:38.649
Throughout the film, he looks half-asleep,
staring absent-mindedly into space, unable

00:01:38.649 --> 00:01:39.779
to feel anything.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.020
At one point, the narrator tells us, "When
you have insomnia, you're never really asleep,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:49.620
and you're never really awake,” as he watches
an infomercial, suggesting that he’s not

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.799
only half-conscious because of insomnia, but
also consumerism.

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:58.509
But more importantly, Fight Club shows a world
of what sociologists call ‘rationalization.’

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:03.099
Rationalization, in this sense, isn’t quite
“using rationality” in the way you might

00:02:03.099 --> 00:02:07.549
think, but “using a certain kind of rationality
to organize society.”

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:12.860
Essentially, rationalization favors efficiency
over tradition, custom, or individual desires.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.930
So, instead of getting your pies made with
love made from the nice baker down the street,

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:22.790
you get them from a factory, where they’re
made on a cold, dead assembly line.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.319
Modern social life is pretty much dominated
by rationalization; it’s organized to accommodate

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:31.150
large numbers of people in the most efficient
way possible.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.260
Everything in the narrator’s life is designed
for a specific purpose, mass produced, and

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.530
unrelentingly predictable — down to his
boss’ tie.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:39.800
"It must've been Tuesday.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.099
He was wearing his cornflower-blue tie."

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:46.680
He even calls his own home a “condo on the
15th floor of a filing cabinet for widows

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.790
and young professionals,” describing his
existence as nothing more than an efficient

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.340
way to store and organize things.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.400
Fight Club shows us that efficiency has its
downsides, especially in a world where “efficient”

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.810
means “efficiently consuming to the point
where, as Tyler Durden says “The things

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.890
you own, end up owning you.”

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:10.170
The narrator describes everything as being
a “copy of a copy of a copy."

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.530
Consumer products, and consumers, are indistinguishable
from each other.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:18.599
To drive the point home, director David Fincher
even put a Starbucks cup in almost every scene

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:19.599
of the film.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:24.390
For philosophers Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer,
this trend of indistinguishable commodities

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:27.170
is a direct result of rationalization.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.870
According to them, everything we’re sold
comes from a standardized ‘menu’ of constructs,

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:36.410
all designed to be as cost-effective as possible
while promoting fake differences.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:40.330
Consider the duvet, which may or may not be
different than a comforter, I don’t know

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.200
— I tried to read about it, gave up.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.220
"Do you know what a duvet is?"

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.280
“A comforter..."

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.500
"It's a blanket.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.780
Just a blanket.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.300
Now, why do guys like you and I know what
a duvet is?

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.360
Is this essential to our survival, in the
hunter-gatherer sense of the word?

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.580
No.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.640
What are we, then?"

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:00.980
"...Consumers?"

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.260
“Right!”

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.860
Some good marketing might make the things
we buy seem different from each other, but

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:10.549
in reality they’re all pretty much the same
- copies of copies of copies.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:13.680
This commodification of all things makes life
pretty dull.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.819
And also kind of fascist.

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:20.290
For Adorno and Horkheimer, and for the characters
in Fight Club, living in a rationalized world

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:25.660
can have a dehumanizing effect, turning everyone
into lonely, alienated drones at the behest

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.760
of consumer capitalism — living life just
following orders, if you will.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.170
Flown around the country on business trips,
the narrator describes the isolation that

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.840
comes with a ‘single-serving’ lifestyle.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.940
Everything is small and disposable.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.160
Even the people he meets.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.660
"The people I meet on each flight -- they're
single-serving friends.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:49.100
Between take-off and landing, we have our
time together, and that's all we get."

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:53.320
After getting in a car accident, he muses
about the dehumanizing effect of his own work,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.620
“I’d never had been in a car accident.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.160
This must have been what all those people
felt like before I filed them as statistics

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:02.310
in my reports."

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.360
So, how do we cope with a single-serving lifestyle?

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.920
One might be tempted to say “get a hobby,
bro.”

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.530
But for Adorno and Horkheimer, ‘amusement’
under modern day capitalism has just become

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:14.440
a ‘prolongation of work’.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:18.670
Hobbies and entertainment serve to rejuvenate
us, so we can go back to work the next day

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:24.110
- but those same things that keep up sane
in our off-hours are still determined by the

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.110
system.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.180
This makes amusement ‘the after-images of
the work process itself’.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.260
In other words,if we aren’t working, we’re
buying mass-produced stuff to help us feel

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.850
better about the fact that we have to go to
work again the next day.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:40.060
"I would flip through catalogues and wonder,
what kind of dining set defines me as a person.”

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:44.220
Or we get the fat sucked out of us which was
caused by our own decadent lifestyle.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.500
"We were selling rich women their own fat
asses back to them."

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:51.370
The narrator's way of coping is still just
an extension of living as a consumer in a

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:52.370
rationalized world.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:58.570
"I had it all, I had a stereo that was very
decent, a wardrobe that was getting very respectable,

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.440
I was close to being complete…"

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.720
Adorno went as far as directly comparing this
kind of mass consumption to fascism.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.810
For him, the mechanism of rationalization
that causes people to blindly throw their

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:15.630
money at big companies, like Starbucks, is
the same mechanism that causes people to throw

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.880
themselves at fascist ideology.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.830
Much like citizens of a fascist state mobilize
under a totalitarian dictator, modern society

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.780
has mobilized under capitalism.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.060
It isn’t until his apartment mysteriously
blows up that the narrator finds a new way

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.110
to resist rationalization.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:36.190
After losing all of his precious Ikea furniture,
he trades in alienating capitalism for a place

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:39.010
in Tyler Durden’s crumbling mansion on Paper
Street.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:42.380
Paper Street is the exact opposite of his
former apartment.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:46.820
While the other one was small, efficient,
and well decorated, Paper Street is needlessly

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:49.080
large, disorganized and dirty.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.890
If his apartment was rational, Paper street
is irrational.

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:57.040
In his new home, he joins Tyler Durden in
his fight against consumer culture.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.650
Tyler, the charming, charismatic leader of
Fight Club, champions a resistance against

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:06.860
oppressive consumerism, offering the narrator
an escape and a way to reclaim his identity.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:11.250
But what does escaping rationalization have
to do with punching people in the face?

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:15.440
Tyler’s resistance takes things a bit further
than your run-of-the-mill critique of capitalism.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.830
He frames consumerism as an assault on masculinity.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:22.780
The men in Fight Club basically fear that
society is turning them into women.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.960
They feel degraded by their jobs.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.410
The Narrator, in this sense, is the perfect
image of an emasculated consumer.

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:33.750
He’s weak and goes to support groups where
he hugs people and cries - You know, girl

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:34.750
stuff.

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.270
At the testicular cancer support group, some
of them have literally become emasculated.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:42.060
One of the participants laments he’s been
cuckolded, as his wife has had the child they

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.700
could never have had with a new man.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:51.670
"She had her first child last week, a girl,
with her, uh… with her new husband."

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:55.140
The film really drives this symbolism home
with the character of Bob.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:00.700
Once a bodybuilder, Bob literally loses his
balls to testicular cancer and grows ‘bitch

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.650
tits,’ as a result of hormone therapy.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.590
"We're still men."

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.180
"Yes, we're men.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:08.740
Men is what we are."

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:14.470
His loss of masculinity results in estrangement
from his family, and he feels completely dehumanized.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.580
"And now I'm bankrupt.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:18.620
I'm divorced.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:25.450
My two grown kids ... won't even return my
phone calls."

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:29.560
The narrator and Tyler also discuss their
absentee fathers, wondering what the role

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.020
of being raised by women has played in their
lives "We're a generation of men raised by

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.020
women.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:38.760
I'm wondering if another woman is really the
answer we need.”

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.460
And Tyler tells the narrator, "You know, man.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.680
It could be worse.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.450
A woman could cut off your penis while you're
sleeping and toss it out the window of a moving

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:45.950
car."

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:46.500
Seriously.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:49.120
This whole movie is about losing your balls
and/or dick.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:53.560
Given the emasculation of modern consumer
society, Fight club is appealing because it

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.500
allows members to express themselves by acting
in a way they can’t in everyday life - being

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.820
really, really violent, which is something
they associate with being men with a capital

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:07.060
“M.” Masculinity through aggression provides
a way for the men to cope with their girly

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.760
day jobs and domestic consumer behavior.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.670
But while Tyler Durden laments the fake kind
of masculinity sold to us by Hollywood, he

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.930
sells them another kind of manliness.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:20.780
The second half of Fight Club is all about
how Tyler Durden has taken all the things

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:25.810
Project Mayhem is supposed to rebel against,
repackaged it, and sold it to his Project

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:26.960
Mayhem stooges.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.500
The members of fight club just become copies
of copies of each other.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:34.170
They shave their heads, call each other “maggots,”
they even lose their names.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.620
"In project mayhem we have no names."

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:41.700
Rebelling against a system that’s made them
faceless drones, they become… well, faceless

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:42.700
drones.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.800
And remember all that stuff about losing your
balls?

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:48.760
Well, testicles under the reign of Tyler aren’t
faring much better either.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:52.890
The movie gives us an early glimpse of what’s
to come as Tyler Durden just repeatedly punches

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.380
this guy in the dick.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.610
Project Mayhem later threatens to cut everyone
else’s balls off.

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:01.290
"You're going to publicly state that there
is no underground group, or these guys are

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:02.620
gonna take your balls.”

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.450
Even its own founder: "You said that if anyone
ever interferes with Project Mayhem, even

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:11.080
you, we gotta get his balls."

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:15.710
For Bob and the narrator, Fight Club has simply
replaced the support groups in their lives,

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:20.120
and they chat about their “Fight Club days”
like one might talk about their weekly support group

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.120
schedules.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.130
"I go Tuesdays and Thursdays."

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.020
"I go Saturday."

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:27.820
Tyler acts like a strong father figure for
the narrator and the rest of Project Mayhem.

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:32.540
"I will carry you, kicking and screaming,
and in the end, you will thank me."

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:36.180
But, like the narrator’s shitty father — “He
left when I was, like, six years old.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.380
Married this other woman, had some other kids.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:40.710
And he, like, did this every six years.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:44.880
He goes to a new city and starts a new family—“
"F*cker's setting up franchises.”

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.320
— Tyler abandons him.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:51.230
The most devoted members of Fight Club and
are put through intense hazing to become members

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:52.630
of Project Mayhem.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.170
Before they can join, prospective members
have to stand outside the Paper Street mansion

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.620
for 3 days without food or shelter, and are
verbally and physically abused.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.130
Those who tough it out are allowed inside
the house and become part of a well-oiled

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:08.590
machine that blows up computer stores, terrorizes
politicians, and sends corporate sculptures

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:10.180
rolling into coffee shops.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:15.270
As Tyler’s movement gains momentum, it transforms
from an after-school activity into a fascist

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.800
cult that might just be as bad as the capitalism
they rebelled against in the first place.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.870
"You are not special.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:25.240
You are not a beautiful or unique snowflake.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:29.960
You are the same decaying organic matter as
everything else.”

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.430
“Tyler built himself an army.”

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:36.770
“We are the all singing all dancing crap
of the world.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:40.290
We are all part of the same compost heap."

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.980
Project Mayhem operates through strict rules
and systematic efficiency.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:48.870
They even rely on capital to run properly,
and fund their activities by making and selling

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:52.320
soap out of human fat stolen from a liposuction
clinic.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.230
The difference is that now instead of reporting
to the demands of their corporate bosses,

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:59.850
members report to their patriarchal, authoritarian
leader: Tyler Durden.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:03.370
"Sooner or later we all became what Tyler
wanted us to be."

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.110
Chapters begin springing up all over the United
States.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.960
The narrator even calls them franchises.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.430
"Tyler had been busy, setting up franchises
all over the country."

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:18.550
The entire time, Project Mayhem has been plotting
toward a master plan: blowing up the offices

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:20.170
of credit card companies.

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:24.130
The narrator discovers this plan, — “Oh
my god.” — and also discovers that he

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:25.430
and Tyler Durden are the same person.

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:28.510
"Is that a pretty accurate description of
our relationship, Tyler?"

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:30.779
"We have just lost cabin pressure."

00:12:30.779 --> 00:12:35.340
Tyler Durden was the manly, virile complement
to the narrator’s emasculated helplessness.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:40.200
And, if we recall, when the narrator says
early on that “and suddenly I realize that

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:45.370
all of this, the guns, the bombs, the revolution,
has got something to something to do with

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:50.450
a girl named Marla Singer," he probably means
his spiral into psychosis began when he needed

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:52.470
to create an alter ego to be with her.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.610
"You were looking for a way to change your
life.

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:57.290
You could not do this on your own.

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:00.900
All the ways you wish you could be, that's
me.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:03.170
I look like you wanna look.

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:04.750
I f*ck like you wanna f*ck.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:09.350
I am smart, capable, and most importantly,
I am free in all the ways you are not.”

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:13.410
He is able to get rid of Durden by shooting
himself in the face, but the damage is already

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:18.240
done - the buildings collapse, supposedly
to reset society’s debt, although I’m

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:19.910
not sure how that actually works.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:24.940
Just like Tyler and the narrator are two sides
of the same person, for Adorno and Horkheimer,

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:28.310
fascism and capitalism are two sides of the
same coin.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:33.750
They argued that both fascism and capitalism
see human beings as numbers instead of individuals

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:36.820
- alienated objects to be used and controlled.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:38.490
So, what do you guys think?

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:39.490
What’s worse?

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:43.290
Succumbing to the allure of Ikea catalogues
and coffee chains, or following orders of

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:46.430
a fascist soap-salesman offering promises
of humanity?

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.860
Let us know what you think in the comments. Or don't.

